\end{mainthm}

full_context:
Now suppose that $\mathfrak{M}$ contains a \textbf{log-atomic} element $T \in \mathfrak{M}^{>1}$, namely such that $\log^{\circ n}(T) \in \mathfrak{M}$ for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$. Then the field $\Rb\wspc{T}$ of \textbf{omega-series} in $T$ is the smallest subclass of $\mathbb{T}$ that contains $T$ and is closed under taking $\exp$, $\log$, and sums like $\sum_{i < \alpha} r_i \mf_i$. $\Rb\wspc{T}$ is automatically of the form $\mathbb{R}\fspc{\mathfrak{O}}_\mathbf{On}$ for some subgroup $\mathfrak{O} \leq \mathfrak{M}$. The choice of $T$ and of ambient $\mathbb{T}$ is irrelevant: all fields of omega-series are isomorphic to each other as fields of transseries.

Just like traditional power series can be differentiated and composed, the same is true for omega-series. There is a unique derivation $f \mapsto f'$ on $\Rb\wspc{T}$ satisfying $T' = 1$, $(e^\gamma)' = e^\gamma \gamma'$ for any $\gamma \in \mathbb{J}$, and which is \textbf{strongly $\mathbb{R}$-linear}, namely
\[ {\left(\sum_{i < \alpha} r_i \mf_i\right)}' = \sum_{i < \alpha} r_i \mf_i'. \]

Likewise, given $g \in \Rb\wspc{T}$ with $g > \mathbb{R}$, there is a unique right composition map $f \mapsto f \circ g$ satisfying $T \circ g = g$, $e^\gamma \circ g = e^{\gamma \circ g}$ for $\gamma \in \mathbb{J}$, and which is strongly $\mathbb{R}$-linear
\[ {\left(\sum_{i < \alpha} r_i \mf_i\right)} \circ g = \sum_{i < \alpha} r_i (\mf_i \circ g). \]
More generally, let $\mathbb{U} = \mathbb{R}\fspc{\mathfrak{N}^{>1}}_{\mathbf{On}}$ be a \textbf{confluent} field of transseries, namely such that for every $x \in \mathbb{U}^{>\mathbb{R}}$ there is $n \in \mathbb{N}$ such that the leading term of $\log^{\circ n}(x)$ is log-atomic. Then for every $x \in \mathbb{U}^{>\mathbb{R}}$ there is a unique map $f \mapsto f \circ x$ satisfying the above properties with $x$ in place of $g$ (while the uniqueness is obvious, the existence is technically challenging, see~\cite{Schmeling2001,BM2019}; while~\cite{BM2019} is stated for $\mathbf{No}$, and seemingly using the assumption `T4', the proof therein only uses confluence). Surreal numbers are an example of confluent field (\cite{BM2018}).

Finally, we remark that composition and derivation are compatible with global $\exp$, namely $\exp(f)' = \exp(f)f'$ and $\exp(f) \circ g = \exp(f \circ g)$ for any $f \in \Rb\wspc{T}$, and with each other, in the sense of the \textbf{chain rule}: for any $f, g \in \Rb\wspc{T}$ with $g > \mathbb{R}$ we have $(f \circ g)' = (f' \circ g)g'$.

However, the properties of \emph{left} composition by some $f \in \Rb\wspc{T}$, namely of the map $x \mapsto f \circ x$, have not been explored as much. Since transseries have been introduced to provide asymptotic expansions of real functions, we expect these map to share at least the most basic properties. For instance, we expect this map to be strictly increasing exactly when $f' > 0$. To the best of my knowledge, this only appears in a barely cited preprint of Edgar~\cite[Prop.\ 4.9]{Edg2009}, for $\mathrm{LE}$-series only, under the heading `Simpler proof needed', and with an `overly-involved proof'.

Addressing Edgar's need, this note gives a short, self-contained proof of monotonicity for general omega-series. We also spell out a small set of inductive assumptions, so that the method can be reapplied more easily to fields larger than $\Rb\wspc{T}$, in particular with an eye to the hyperseries of~\cite{BHK2021}, which generalise transseries by adding transexponential functions. From now on, let $\mathbb{U}$ be a fixed confluent field of transseries, for instance $\mathbb{U} = \mathbf{No}$.

However, the properties of \emph{left} composition by some $f \in \OM$, namely of the map $x \mapsto f \circ x$, have not been explored as much. Since transseries have been introduced to provide asymptotic expansions of real functions, we expect these map to share at least the most basic properties. For instance, we expect this map to be strictly increasing exactly when $f' > 0$. To the best of my knowledge, this only appears in a barely cited preprint of Edgar~\cite[Prop.\ 4.9]{Edg2009}, for $\LE$-series only, under the heading `Simpler proof needed', and with an `overly-involved proof'.

Addressing Edgar's need, this note gives a short, self-contained proof of monotonicity for general omega-series. We also spell out a small set of inductive assumptions, so that the method can be reapplied more easily to fields larger than $\OM$, in particular with an eye to the hyperseries of~\cite{BHK2021}, which generalise transseries by adding transexponential functions. From now on, let $\Ub$ be a fixed confluent field of transseries, for instance $\Ub = \No$.

Since $\OM$ is generated from $T$, a naive proof by induction would be as follows: the conclusion is true for the base case $f = T$, $f = \log(T)$, \ldots; if $f = \sum_{i < \alpha} r_i e^{\gamma_i}$, we are free to assume the conclusion for each map $x \mapsto \gamma_i \circ x$, and we would like to deduce that $x \mapsto f \circ x$ is also monotonic. This works very well for $f \in \Jb_\Of^{>0}$, and we do so in \Cref{monotonicity-J}, but the inductive assumption falls short for the general case. We also need the following very weak form of mean value property: given $\gamma \in \Jb_\Of^{>0}$ and $x, y \in \Ub$ with $x > y > \Rb$, we have
\[ \frac{\gamma \circ x - \gamma \circ y}{x - y} \preceq \gamma' \circ y \quad \text{at least when} \quad \gamma \circ x - \gamma \circ y \preceq 1 \text{ or } (\gamma' \circ y)(x - y) \prec 1, \]
where $a \preceq b$ means $|a| \leq n|b|$ for some $n \in \Nb$ and $a \prec b$ means $n|a| < |b|$ for all $n \in \Nb$. The condition is almost trivial to verify in the base case of $T$, $\log(T)$, $\ldots$, as the associated functions $x \mapsto \log^{\circ n}(x)$ are all strictly increasing and \emph{concave}. The rest of the induction is then relatively straightforward (see \Cref{weak-mvp}), and it leads to a fairly short proof of \Cref{monotonicity}.

\begin{maincor}\label{ivp}
 For all $f \in \OM$, the function $g \mapsto f \circ g$ for $g \in \OM^{>\Rb}$ has the intermediate value property: if $f \circ g \leq a \leq f \circ \tilde{g}$, there is $h \in \OM$ between $g$ and $\tilde{g}$ such that $f \circ g = a$.
\end{maincor}

\begin{prop}\label{monotonicity-J}
 For all $\gamma \in \Jb^{>0}$, the map $x \mapsto \gamma \circ x$ is strictly increasing.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
 Fix some $x, y \in \Ub$ with $x > y > \Rb$. We prove by induction on $\ER$ that $\gamma \circ x > \gamma \circ y$. The statement is true for $\ER(\gamma) = 0$: the identity function and the function $\log$ are strictly increasing on $\Ub^{>0}$, and so are their iterates.

\textbf{Case $f > \Rb$.} Let $n \in \Nb$ be such that $\log^{\circ n}(T) \circ f = \log^{\circ n}(f) = \log^{\circ k}(T) + \varepsilon$ for some $k \in \Nb$ and with $\varepsilon \prec 1$ (such $n$, $k$ exist because $\OM$ is confluent; for a direct proof, note that the leading monomial of the omega-series $\log^{\circ n}(f)$ must have decreasing rank until one reaches $\log^{\circ k}(T)$ for some $k$). Note that
 \[ \log^{\circ n}(T) \circ f \circ \exp^{\circ k}(T) = T + \overline{\varepsilon} \]
 where $\overline{\varepsilon} = \varepsilon \circ \exp^{\circ k}(T) \prec 1$. By the previous case, the function $x \mapsto x + (\overline{\varepsilon} \circ x)$ is strictly increasing. Since the functions $\log$ and $\exp$ are also strictly increasing on $\Ub^{>\Rb}$, then so is the function $x \mapsto f \circ x$.

\textbf{Case $f \not > \Rb$}. Since by assumption $f' > 0$, we cannot have $f < \Rb$, so there must be some $r \in \Rb$ such that $f - r \prec 1$, and moreover $f - r < 0$. It follows that $g = -\frac{1}{f - r} > \Rb$. By the previous case, the function $x \mapsto g \circ x$ is strictly increasing, thus so is the function
 \[ x \mapsto -\frac{1}{g \circ x} + r = f \circ x. \qedhere \]
\end{proof}

\begin{cor}\label{radius-of-convergence}
 For all $f \in \OM$, if $f \not\asymp T^k$ for any $k \in \Nb$, then eventually the sequence $(f^{(k)} \circ x)\delta^{k}$ is strictly $\prec$-decreasing when $\delta \prec x$ and $(f^\dagger \circ x)\delta \prec 1$, and weakly $\prec$-increasing otherwise.
\end{cor}
\begin{proof}
 We apply \Cref{iter-der-dagger} to the ratio between two successive elements of the sequence is
 \[ \frac{(f^{(k+1)} \circ x)\delta^{k+1}}{(f^{(k)} \circ x)\delta^{k}} = \left(\left(\frac{f^{(k+1)}}{f^{(k)}}\right)\circ x\right) \delta = ({(f^{(k)})}^\dagger \circ x)\delta. \]

\begin{mainthm}[Monotonicity]\label{monotonicity}
    For all $f \in \OM$, the function $x \mapsto f \circ x$ for $x \in \Ub^{>\Rb}$ is strictly increasing if $f' > 0$, strictly decreasing if $f' < 0$, and constant if $f' = 0$.
\end{mainthm}

\begin{prop}\label{monotonicity-J}
  For all $\gamma \in \Jb^{>0}$, the map $x \mapsto \gamma \circ x$ is strictly increasing.
\end{prop}

\begin{lem}\label{weak-mvp}
  For all $\gamma \in \Jb^{>0}$ and all $x, y \in \Ub$ with $x > y > \Rb$, we have:
  \begin{enumerate}
      \item\label{weak-mvp-small} if $\gamma \circ x - \gamma \circ y \preceq 1$, then $\frac{\gamma \circ x - \gamma \circ y}{x - y} \preceq \gamma' \circ y$,
      \item\label{weak-mvp-large} if $\gamma \circ x - \gamma \circ y \succeq 1$, then $(\gamma' \circ y)(x - y) \succeq 1$.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{lem}

