In the construction of $T$ in~\eqref{eq:T_general}, the choice of $\mathcal{L}$ is critical. Indeed, for an arbitrary $\mathcal{L}$ there is no reason for $T$ to be contracting near $\bar{u}$, especially if $\tau$ is somewhat large. However, since the Fréchet derivative of $T$ at $\bar{u}$ is
\begin{align*}
DT(\bar{u})h(t) = \int_0^t U(t,s) D_u\gamma(s,\bar{u}(s))h(s) \mathrm{d} s,\qquad t\in[0,\tau],
\end{align*}
a natural choice is to take $\mathcal{L}(t)=DF(\bar{u}(t))$, because it yields $D_u\gamma(t,\bar{u}(t)) = 0$ for all $t$, hence $DT(\bar{u}) = 0$. In that case, provided $\bar{u}$ is an accurate enough approximate solution, i.e., that $T(\bar{u})-\bar{u}$ is small enough, $T$ should indeed be a contraction on a small neighborhood of $\bar{u}$. Nonetheless, actually proving this requires getting somewhat sharp quantitative estimates on the evolution operator $\mathcal{U}$, which is a challenging task, especially because $\mathcal{L}(t)=DF(\bar{u}(t))$ depends on time in a non-trivial way via the approximate solution $\bar{u}$. Despite these challenges, this approach was successfully used in~\cite{TakMizKubOis17,TakLesJaqOka22,DucLesTak25}.\footnote{To be precise, these works take advantage of the parabolic/dissipative structure of~\eqref{eq:PDE} to use a simple approximation of $DF(\bar{u}(t))$ for the high frequencies, similar to the one that is going to be used in this paper, but for the low frequencies they do indeed take $\mathcal{L}(t)=DF(\bar{u}(t))$.} Very similar ideas, but presented with a different viewpoint, can be found in~\cite{HasKimMinNak19,HasKinNak20}, see also~\cite[Chapter 5]{NakPluWat19}.

In this paper we take an intermediate path, namely we take for $\mathcal{L}$ a piece-wise constant approximation of $DF(\bar{u}(t))$. The rational behind this choice is the following. We recall that, when $\mathcal{L}$ is taken equal to $DF(\bar{u}(t))$, we have $DT(\bar{u}) = 0$. However, this is much stronger than what we actually need in order to apply the contraction mapping, namely that $\left\Vert DT(u)\right\Vert \leq C < 1$ for all $u$ in some small neighborhood of $\bar{u}$. Therefore, there is some room to play with in the definition of $\mathcal{L}$, which should be used to obtain an evolution operator $\mathcal{U}$ which is as easy to analyze as possible, and our choice of $\mathcal{L}$ is guided by these considerations.
Indeed, provided the subdivision in time is fine enough, choosing $\mathcal{L}$ to be a piecewise constant approximation of $DF(\bar{u}(t))$ will cause the map $T$ to still be contracting around $\bar{u}$ for long integration times $\tau$. Moreover, by having $\mathcal{L}$ piece-wise time-independent we retain the ability to get an explicit description of the evolution operator $\mathcal{U}$, for which estimates are then much easier to obtain.

